Early Onset Prediction for Rotors in Vortex Ring State
Joel McQuaid1; Amir Kolaei, Ph.D.2; Götz Bramesfeld, Ph.D., P.Eng3; and Paul Walsh, Ph.D., P.Eng4
Abstract: The OpenFOAM version 5.0 CFD package was used to investigate detection methods for a small quadrotor drone entering the vortex-ring state. The vortex-ring state causes increased thrust peak-to-mean fluctuations that can be partially attributed to a breakdown of the stable helical wake vortex into a single vortex-ring trapped around the rotor tip. These thrust fluctuations cause significant flight control issues for rotorcraft. Detection of a rotorcraft approaching this flight condition becomes important to avoid any loss of vehicle control. A method for detecting the onset of the vortex-ring state is proposed using various differential pressure measurements on the quadrotor. It has been shown that, by monitoring the pressure difference between the top of the quadrotor body and the bottom of one of the quadrotor legs, a pressure drop can be observed just prior to the onset of the vortex-ring state. This pressure drop was shown to occur during descending flight regimes and may prove to be an effective pre-vortex-ring state warning system. DOI: 10.1061/(ASCE)AS.1943-5525.0001194. © 2020 American Society of Civil Engineers.
Introduction
The vortex-ring state is a highly unsteady and turbulent flight regime that a rotor encounters when descending into its downwash. Descending into the downwash of a rotor beyond a certain point causes the stable helical wake vortex structure to collapse into a single strong vortex ring that encompasses the rotor tips (Rodriguez 2007; Rumsey 1987). A reduction in the effective angle of attack of the rotor tip, in combination with an increased inflow velocity into the rotor plane, causes reductions in thrust forces and an increase in thrust force oscillations about a mean thrust value (Brand et al. 2007; Azuma and Obata 1968; Betzina 2001; Mullen and Bernini 2016). The ability to predict the onset of a vortex-ring state remains an important research venture in both the computational and experimental fields of rotor aerodynamics. Avoiding the vortex-ring state in rotorcraft currently requires either generous safety margins placed around the descent-velocity range of a vehicle or an extremely comprehensive set of experimental data over a range of flight maneuvers to better predict these phenomena (Varnes 1999). Even these results are susceptible to experimental errors or deviations in flight, such as when wind is present. Therefore, being able to predict the onset of the vortex-ring state through an inflight monitoring system would provide a
significant benefit to safety for rotorcraft operators and flight vehicles. The unsteady nature of the vortex-ring state renders the use of classical momentum theory approaches for modeling this state to be ineffective (Taamallah 2010; Leishman 2000; Ribera and Celi 2007; McCormick 1979). In this work, the vortex-ring state is defined using two boundaries—the upper and lower boundarywhich is based on the magnitude of the descent rate. The upper boundary is defined as having a smaller magnitude of descent or being closer to the hovering condition, whereas the lower boundary is at a higher magnitude of descent. These boundaries are said to be in the range of hovering (upper boundary) to a descent of twice the rotor-induced velocity at hover (lower boundary), Vh. As the rotorcraft continues to increase its descent rate past the lower vortex-ring state boundary, it enters the post-vortex-ring state flight regime commonly referred to as the windmill brake state, and momentum theory can be reapplied (Taamallah 2010). The experimental work of Wolkovitch (1972) shows that a descent velocity magnitude of 0.707Vh is the vortex-ring state upper boundary at which faster descent rates cause the vortex-ring state to begin. The velocity, Vh, is defined as
