power oscillations if the collective pitch angle of the rotor is high enough (Azuma and Obata 1968). The magnitude at which the vortex-ring state affects the thrust of a rotor and the velocity at which it begins has also been indicated to depend on the rotor blade loading and the spanwise blade twist distribution (Brown et al. 2002). In terms of flow visualization, a breakdown in the stable helical rotor wake vortex may be observed to shift into a single donut-shaped wake vortex that is trapped around the rotor-plane tip. Flow visualization has been used both experimentally through smoke/water tunnels or numerically through Q-criterion and vorticity to identify the vortex-ring state. Mitigation and avoidance strategies for the vortex-ring state continue to be the main points of interest within the realm of rotorcraft flight. Current measures are limited to avoiding purely descending flights and/or employing alternate flight maneuvers that assist in keeping the shed wake vortex away from the rotor plane during descent. If descent angles are minimized, such as less than or equal to 30° descent angles, the vortex-ring state is nonexistent at virtually all descent speeds (Taamallah 2010). The lack of vortexring state effects at low descent angles is because of the forward velocity preventing a wake vortex buildup in the rotor plane. Betzina (2001) supported this claim by reporting that entering the vortex-ring state is not possible at descent angles less than 20°. For this study, the authors used a tiltrotor in helicopter mode in the National Aeronautics and Space Administration’s (NASA) Ames wind tunnel (Betzina 2001). Employing flight maneuvers is currently the optimal way for rotorcraft to avoid or minimize the impact of the vortex-ring state on flight safety. One possible maneuver is to descend into the windmill brake state through a rapid increase in descent speed, which requires sufficient ground clearance. Dziubinski and Stalewski (2007) used flow visualization of their results to indicate that this maneuver effectively pushes the vortex ring above the rotor plane and, consequently, allows for thrust to recover (Dziubinski and Stalewski 2007). Vice versa, a decrease in descent speed is also capable of helping a rotorcraft exit vortex-ring state, as indicated by Grzegorczyk (2013); however, this results in slower thrust recovery due to the strength of the vortex-ring. Spiraled flight patterns or increased forward speed maneuvers are two other approaches that may be used (Taamallah 2010; Chenglong et al. 2015). Mullen and Bernini (2016) used a descent velocity sweep to study the rapid thrust reduction experienced by the rotor on entering the vortex-ring state followed by the steady thrust recovery in the post-vortex-ring state regime (Mullen and Bernini 2016). Ahlin and Brown (2005) demonstrated that rotor thrust recovery begins to occur before the rotor wake stabilizes fully into its windmill brake state structure (Ahlin and Brown 2005). The extension of vortex-ring state effects on rotor performance has been largely limited to the single rotor and helicopter models, and little investigation has occurred on its impact on multirotor systems (Foster and Hartman 2017). Foster and Hartman (2017) studied the quadrotor’s performance under varying descent velocities and descent angles to evaluate the impact on thrust fluctuations, mean thrust, and rotor interference. They concluded that a quadrotor experiences unintended pitch and roll moments potentially due to frequency and phase differences resulting from the individual thrust fluctuation of each rotor. Furthermore, they proposed that these uncontrolled moments may be a result of rotor interactional effects. The purpose of this study is to demonstrate how pressure differential measurements may be used to predict when a rotorcraft is about to cross the vortex-ring state upper boundary. This work begins with a validation study of a single rotor to ensure that the OpenFOAM computational model is capable of accurately
calculating rotor performance versus the experimental data. Next, the same OpenFOAM numerical methodology was applied to a quadrotor drone to investigate the ability of the computational model to capture the relevant vortex-ring state effects. The ability to model the vortex-ring state regime was demonstrated through a descent velocity sweep from hover to a high descent rate. Then, the rotor thrust fluctuation and mean thrust versus descent rate and flow visualizations of the rotor wake were used to indicate that the quadrotor entered into the vortex-ring state. Finally, three pressure ports were defined around the drone fuselage, and pressure differential measurements were calculated to present a means of predicting the onset of the vortex-ring state.
Numerical Methodology
This section presents the computational model formulation used for the single-rotor validation study. The results obtained from the OpenFOAM model are compared with previously obtained experimental data in a low-speed, closed-circuit wind tunnel. A two-mesh grid study is then performed and validated using the Richardson extrapolation. The purpose of this section is to demonstrate the ability of the proposed model to compute realistic rotor performance versus the experimental results. A numerical model capable of analyzing the unsteady aerodynamics of a quadrotor was formulated in OpenFOAM with a sliding mesh interface to allow for rotor rotation. A Reynolds-averaged Navier–Stokes (RANS) formulation has been indicated to be unsuitable for modeling temporal aerodynamic phenomena based on the work by Westbrook-Netherton and Toomer (2015). Therefore, a detached eddy simulation (DES) approach was used in combination with RANS modeling to capture any transient effects occurring during the vortex-ring state (Yoon et al. 2016; Weerasinghe and Monasor 2017). A DES formulation provides a more computationally efficient approach because RANS modeling is used in the near-wall treatment, whereas large eddy simulation (LES) modeling is used in the freestream region to better capture the wake structure. This methodology helps maintain better usage of computational resources versus a full LES model while providing better accuracy than a purely RANS model. The presented numerical results are based on an unsteady simulation run for five revolutions for a total of 1,800 timesteps (1 timestep per 1° of rotor rotation). Convergence was reached by the end of the fourth revolution, allowing for the final revolution to be time-averaged. The numerical results were validated using a comparison to the experimental results obtained in the large subsonic wind tunnel of Ryerson University. Details regarding the experimental results can be found in Kolaei et al. (2018). Validation was performed only on the single-rotor model to determine the grid density required to accurately compute the rotor aerodynamic performance. The subsequent quadrotor model was not investigated through a validation study, but it maintains nearly identical grid density and spacing. The only difference between the validation model and the quadrotor model is the physical geometry under investigation. A one-rotor 46-cm (18-in.) rotor geometry was used as the validation model. This rotor geometry has been labeled the “T-motor rotor” or “T-motor” within the Ryerson Applied Aerodynamics Laboratory of Flight (RAALF) and is referred to as such for the remainder of this manuscript. Fig. 1 plots the T-motor chord and twist distribution from rotor hub to rotor tip, where c = airfoil chord length; R = rotor radius; r = distance from rotor hub to a given point on the rotor blade; and β = rotor pitch angle. More details on the rotor geometry can be found in Kolaei et al. (2018).
