were estimated to be no higher than Mach 0.23, thus rendering the compressibility effects negligible. Model patches surrounding the T-motor were set as arbitrary mesh interfaces (AMI), which allowed the modeling of the rotor rotation and served as an interpolation interface between the rotor and the remainder of the freestream gas path. Boundary patches at the model extremities were set to velocity-inlet and pressure-outlet conditions depending on the need to model climbing or descending cases. Element sizing and transition rates within the mean flow and in the boundary layer play an important role in the accurate computation of the rotor performance. Element sizing is at its maximum at the outermost subregion and grows steadily more refined toward the rotor itself to better predict viscous interactions within the boundary layer and complex flow structures that are expected to occur during the vortex-ring state. Boundary layer meshing involves 20 layers of structured prism elements that are normal to the wall surface, as indicated in Fig. 3. The total grid refinement of all subregions was 40 million elements. The two-equation, k-ω shear stress transport (SST) model was implemented for predicting viscous interactions. The turbulent kinetic energy (kT∞ ) and turbulent frequency (ω∞) were adjusted to match the values expected within the wind tunnel at which the experimental results were obtained. Based on hotwire anemometry tests, the turbulent intensity was measured to be approximately 0.25%, as observed in Kolaei et al. (2018). The turbulent frequency and turbulent kinetic energy are functions of turbulent intensity, Tu∞, freestream velocity (U∞), kinematic viscosity (ν), and turbulent kinematic viscosity (νT)
