as also performed to validate the model through a two-mesh study. The first mesh, herein referred to as the “baseline” model, represents the previously mentioned grid used to compare with the experimental data. The second grid, herein referred to as the “refined” model, served as the comparative solution. The grid densities for the baseline and refined models were 40 and 80 million cells, respectively. A Richardson extrapolation was performed between the two grids to estimate the grid convergence index (GCI) value of both grids and the expected error of both models. The results of this study are provided in Table 1. The predicted exact solution at an infinite grid density is a thrust and power coefficient of 0.01690 and 2.54e-3, respectively. The error between the baseline model and the predicted exact solution on the thrust and torque is 7.579% and 7.712%, respectively, which yields a good tradeoff between accuracy and the computational efficiency of running a smaller model.
