Quadrotor Computational Model
A computational model was developed to resemble a typical quadrotor configuration based on geometric files provided by Aeryon Labs Inc. (now part of FLIR systems). A finalized model rendering is provided in Fig. 6. The body has a diameter of approximately 24 cm (9.5 in.), and the quadrotor has been fitted with the Aeryon185 rotor, which is 47 cm (18.5-in.) in diameter. Fig. 7 presents the rotor chord and twist distributions as a function of the distance from the rotor hub. The computational model was again designed to manage grid spacing in the freestream and boundary layer regions. Fig. 8 provides a schematic for the computational model and presents model sizing based on the rotor radius, R, and the boundary conditions. Fig. 9 presents the same model in an isometric view to better present the model geometry and domain. Model sizing was made relative to the quadrotor centroid. The model used in this section maintains the same grid density to the T-motor model previously validated. As a result, no additional grid independence study was performed on this model. Additional subregions were modeled into the computational schematic to better control grid density and more
gradual grid spacing that allows for a more effective detached eddy simulation (DES). The Aeryon185 is mounted to the drone arm, as observed in Fig. 10. The interface between the rotor subdomain and freestream domain is the location of the AMI patch for the sliding mesh interface. The downwash at hover was determined using momentum theory [Eq. (1)], and the thrust was predicted at the near hover conditions of a 0.22 climbing velocity ratio by the computational model. Boundary conditions were set to replicate sea level conditions, and the rotor speed was 3,000 rpm. A velocity ratio of 0 (hover model) was not investigated due to numerical instability caused by no initial inflowing velocity. With the computed numerical solution, the rotor hover-induced velocity, Vh, can be calculated as 4.65 m=s.
