Similar to the validation study, a time-averaged solution can be confirmed by examining the convergence history of the rotor thrust loading, as observed in Fig. 11 for a 7.5 m=s descent rate. The solution is virtually unchanging after a time of 0.08 s, when the time-averaging begins. Any change in the time-averaged thrust solution due to running the case for longer than five revolutions was assumed to be insignificant. Entry into the vortex-ring state was identified through a number of methods, including thrust fluctuations as a function of the descent rate, flow visualization depicting the rotor wake breakdown, and thrust degradation on entry into the vortex-ring state. Fig. 12 outlines the evolution of rotor thrust fluctuations as the descent rate was increased. Thrust fluctuations were calculated as a percentage of the maximum thrust fluctuation about the timeaveraged value for the final revolution. The peak thrust fluctuation was 15.17% and occurred at a descent rate of 4 m=s (VZ=Vh 1⁄4 −0.86). This result aligns with Wolkovitch (1972) as well as a
number of other researchers as being the vortex-ring state upper boundary. Wake breakdown on entry into the vortex-ring state was investigated next through the two-dimensional vorticity contours at different descent rates. The contour plane passes through the rotor hub to show the rotor wake. Furthermore, the contour plot was reflected about the symmetry axis to present an approximation to the flow structure that may be observed by a two-rotor model. However, the reader is reminded that this is only a single-rotor model in combination with the drone fuselage; therefore, any interactional effects between the rotors are not present in the flow contour. Fig. 13 depicts a flow structure around the quadrotor at a 4.5 m=s descent rate (VZ=Vh 1⁄4 −0.98) that lacks any coherent flow structure representing the wake breakdown characteristic of the vortex-ring state. In contrast, at a slightly higher descent rate of 5 m=s (VZ=Vh 1⁄4 −1.08), Fig. 14 clearly indicates how the wake structure has restabilized into a single vortex ring concentrated around the rotor tips.
