The vortex-ring state flight regime was finally identified using the thrust profile with respect to the descent-velocity ratio. Figs. 15(a and b) plot the descent velocity sweep results for the thrust coefficient, CT, versus the velocity ratio, VZ=Vh, which is the climbing/descending velocity normalized with the rotor-induced velocity at hover. Rapid degradation in the rotor thrust marks the beginning of the vortex-ring state that, as expected, occurred close to the rotor-induced velocity at hover (VZ=Vh 1⁄4 1). Maximum thrust loss due to the vortex-ring state occurs at a descent rate of 4.5 m=s (Vz=Vh 1⁄4 −0.97), which marks the beginning of this phenomenon. These results imply that the vortex-ring state occurs at 4.5 m=s and faster descent rates and is nonexistent at slower descent rates. The numerical model appears to detect a marginal
change in the power coefficient across the vortex-ring state flight regime. The authors can conclude that the vortex-ring state flight regime of the current configuration occurs from a VZ=Vh of −0.8 to −1.1.
Vortex-Ring State Prediction by Pressure Differential Analysis
The ability to predict the early onset of the vortex-ring state becomes critical for the safe operation of rotorcraft when descending. One proposed method is to investigate pressure differentials over the quadrotor fuselage to locate a warning sign for quadrotors that are descending too close to the critical descent rate. Three differential pressures were investigated in this study. Three pressure-port locations are defined to assess the three differential pressures. The locations for the pressure-ports can be observed in Fig. 16. Port 1 is located at the top of the quadrotor fuselage and is aligned with a vertical axis that passes through the body centroid. Port 1 is approximately 5-cm above the fuselage surface, conceivably mounted on a sting. Port 2 is located 5-cm below the quadrotor fuselage, also in alignment with the same vertical axis through the body’s centroid. Port 3 is located just below one of the legs of the quadrotor landing gear. Important to note is that although all three ports are indicated on the same plane in Fig. 16, port 3 is offset from this plane by approximately 10 cm, directed into the page. The notation for the measured pressure difference, ΔPij, between point i and j is
