The effectiveness of differential pressure monitoring can be shown on a plot of pressure versus descent rate, as in Fig. 17. The time-averaged results show the pressure differentials ΔP12, ΔP13, and ΔP23 across the previously tested descent velocity sweep. The ΔP12 pressure differential picks up an approximate 40-Pa pressure drop when in the vortex-ring state; however, minimal pressure shifts are observed before the vortex-ring state takes effect. This pressure drop is problematic because the indicative pressure drop only occurs once the aircraft has already entered the vortex-ring state regime, and it may already be too late for preventive measures. Alternatively, ΔP13 indicates a more significant pressure drop that occurs at descent velocities before the vortexring state begins and continues decreasing until just after the vortex-ring state ends. This pressure differential presents a potentially viable option for predicting the vortex-ring state in a purely descending flight maneuver. Similarly, the ΔP23 also shows an indicative pressure drop prior to the onset of the vortex-ring state; however, the trend is marred with a subsequent increase in pressure immediately after which may make the pressure differential more difficult to monitor inflight, whereas the ΔP13 measurement continues to decrease.
