Conclusion
A prediction strategy for the onset of the vortex-ring state was proposed through pressure differential measurements surrounding a computational quadrotor model. The computational model was validated using a single rotor during climbing flight relative to the experimental data obtained in a subsonic wind tunnel and through a grid independence study. The quadrotor numerical model then employed an identical grid density to the validation model and was tested at various descent velocities to examine the computational model’s ability to calculate the vortex-ring state regime. Finally, a prediction method was proposed by defining various pressure ports around the quadrotor fuselage. A pressure differential probe between the top and bottom of the quadrotor body provided no indication of the onset of the vortex-ring state until the quadrotor had already entered this flight regime. However, a differential pressure probe between the top of the drone body and the bottom of one of the quadrotor legs indicates a pressure drop well before the vortex-ring state takes effect. This result indicates that an early warning system of the vortex-ring state for quadrotors may be possible by measuring pressure differentials in this setup. Continued study of different flight paths must also be performed to determine whether the proposed system is suitable for other flight conditions. Furthermore, the implemented system needs to recognize the difference between a pressure drop due to changes in airflow conditions versus a pressure drop caused by the onset of the vortex-ring state. This distinction could be made by correlating a measured pressure drop with the vertical velocity component of the quadrotor such that the vortex-ring state is predicted only if the drone descends in combination with a measured pressure drop.
